  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Mangbetu\"\nsubtitle: \"Democratic Republic of the Congo\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Mangbetu, a Central Sudanic ethnic group. |\n| Where | Haut-Uele in northeastern Democratic Republic of the Congo. |\n| How many | Not provided. |\n| Language | Mangbetu, also called Nemangbetu. |\n| Religion | Not described. |\n| Known for | Elaborate art and music · Mangbetu harp or guitar · Iron and copper work · Distinctive pottery and figures · Former head-elongation practice called Lipombo |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Mangbetu live in northeastern Congo. Their culture is known for developed art, music, metalwork, pottery, textiles, body adornment, and royal imagery. Their language is Mangbetu, also called Nemangbetu.\n\n## Material culture\n\nMangbetu material culture includes decorated textiles, pottery with human heads, iron and copper objects, carved wood, musical instruments, and elaborate hairstyles and adornment.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nMangbetu textiles used plant fibers, dye, and strong geometric designs.\n\n- **Special-occasion garment** (*Egbe*) — An Egbe was a plant-fiber garment made by aristocratic women and decorated with bold geometric motifs.\n- **Back skirt or sitting pad** (*negbe*) — A negbe was made from compressed leaf or bark and vegetal fibers in woven, zigzag, crisscrossing, or chequered patterns.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nThe sources describe special-occasion women’s garments, buttock covers, fiber skirts, and a feathered dance-cap.\n\n- **Special-occasion garment** (*Egbe*) — An Egbe was worn over a skirt and tied to a girdle with a frontal apron.\n- **Buttock cover** (*negbe*) — A negbe was a vegetal-fiber back skirt or sitting pad.\n- **Feathered dance-cap** — A dance-cap was made from an elephant’s ear and feathers.\n- **Fiber apron** — A carved male figure is shown wearing a vegetal-fiber apron.\n- **Fiber skirt** — Another male figure is shown wearing a fiber skirt around the waist.\n\n### Architecture\n\nWestern photographs and films documented Mangbetu architecture and royal courts, but the sources give no building details.\n\n- **Royal courts** — Western photographs and films documented Mangbetu royal courts.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nMangbetu makers produced weapons, tools, pottery, musical objects, and carved wooden goods.\n\n- **Iron weapons and tools** — Iron objects included knives, axes, arm-daggers, swords, arrow-heads, spear-heads, spears, and a chisel-shaped currency blade.\n- **Elephant-ivory horn** — A musical horn was made from elephant ivory and was described as used in war.\n- **Head-shaped pottery vessels** — Pottery vessels could have human heads, tall headdresses, hats or coiffures, earrings, loop handles, spouts, and string-like ornament.\n- **Wooden household objects** — Wooden objects included stools, seats, boxes, pipes, combs, and fly-whisks.\n- **Carved figures** — Mangbetu wooden objects included carved figures.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nMangbetu adornment included shell and seed necklaces, hair-pins, earrings, elaborate hairstyles, and the former Lipombo practice.\n\n- **Shell-and-seed necklace** — Necklaces used pale bluish shell discs and brown seeds strung on vegetable-fiber cord.\n- **Hair-pins** — Hair-pins were made from ivory, wood, or both.\n- **Brass-wire earring** — A pottery figure shows a human head wearing a brass-wire earring.\n- **Elaborate hairstyles** — Women’s hairstyles were elaborate, and figures often showed the traditional elongated-head appearance.\n- **Head elongation** (*Lipombo*) — Babies’ heads were formerly wrapped tightly with cloth in the practice called Lipombo.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nMangbetu music is highly developed and is especially associated with the Mangbetu harp or guitar.\n\n- **Mangbetu harp or guitar** — The Mangbetu harp or guitar has a bow-shaped form, a wooden neck, a skin-covered resonator, sound holes, pegs, strings, and sometimes a human head or figure at the neck.\n- **Named harp record** (*kundi*) — A Museum of World Culture catalogue includes a musical record identified as kundi.\n- **Ivory musical horn** — A musical horn made from elephant ivory is recorded as a Mangbetu instrument.\n- **Seed or metal rattle** — A rattle had a plaited vine-leaf cage containing dried seeds or metal clappers.\n- **Conical ivory whistles** — Museum records include conical ivory whistles.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nMangbetu dances were documented by Western photographers and filmmakers, but no named dance or theatre form is given.\n\n- **Black-and-white dance mask** — A wooden dance mask painted black and white is held in a museum collection.\n- **Feathered dance-cap** — A dance-cap made from an elephant’s ear and feathers is recorded.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nThe sources give no festival calendar but record Lipombo and political events linked to royal rule.\n\n- **Head-wrapping practice** (*Lipombo*) — Babies’ heads were traditionally wrapped tightly with cloth in Lipombo, which declined in the 1950s with westernization.\n- **Royal courts and dances** — The sources mention royal courts and dances.\n- **Political expansion through wives** — The sources mention local wives used in King Nabiembali’s political expansion.\n- **Independent kingdoms** — Independent kingdoms were established after King Nabiembali’s death in 1859.\n\n## Foodways\n\nThe sources do not describe Mangbetu foods or cooking, but they record a word for fish.\n\n- **Fish** (*ná-mutali*) — The language source gives ná-mutali as a word meaning fish.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nMangbetu and Meegye oral literature includes tale texts and works about storytelling and peace-building.\n\n- **Narrative connector** (*bhe*) — The connective bhe marks discourse development in Meegye narrative texts.\n- **Leopard and Azapane tales** — Robert Guy McKee’s work includes Mangbetu Tales of Leopard and Azapane: Trickster as Resistance Hero.\n- **Death-compensation studies** — The sources include work on Meegye-Mangbetu death compensations as intergroup rites of passage.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nMangbetu is a Central Sudanic language with several named dialects and links to regional trade languages.\n\n- **Mangbetu language** (*Nemangbetu*) — Mangbetu, also called Nemangbetu, is spoken in northeastern Congo.\n- **Alternative people names** (*Amangbetu*) — The speakers and language are also identified as Amangbetu.\n- **Alternative people names** (*Kingbetu*) — The speakers and language are also identified as Kingbetu.\n- **Alternative people names** (*Mambetto*) — The speakers and language are also identified as Mambetto.\n- **Most widely understood dialect** (*Medje*) — Medje is the most widely understood Mangbetu dialect.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Egbe* — Special-occasion garment\n- *negbe* — Back skirt or sitting pad\n- *Lipombo* — Former head-wrapping practice\n- *kundi* — Named musical record\n- *ná-mutali* — Fish\n- *bhe* — Narrative connective\n- *Nemangbetu* — Another name for Mangbetu\n- *Amangbetu* — Name for the people or language\n- *Kingbetu* — Name for the people or language\n- *Mambetto* — Name for the people or language\n- *Medje* — Most widely understood dialect\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- Christopher Ehret, *The Civilizations of Africa: A History to 1800*, University of Virginia Press, 2002.\n- Stefano Allovio, *La foresta di alleanze: Popoli e riti in Africa equatoriale*, Editori Laterza, 1999.\n- Curtis A. Keim, *Mistaking Africa: Curiosities and Inventions of the American Mind*, Basic Civitas Books, 1999.\n- Curtis A. Keim, *Precolonial Mangbetu Rule: Political and Economic Factors in Nineteenth-Century Mangbetu History (Northeast Zaire)*, Ph.D. dissertation, Indiana University, 1979.\n- Robert Guy McKee, *Meje-Mangbetu (Northeastern Zaire) Death Compensations as Intergroup Rites of Passage: A Structural, Cultural and Linguistic Study*, Ph.D. dissertation, University of Rochester, 1995.\n- [Mangbetu people](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mangbetu_people)\n- [Mangbetu language](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mangbetu_language)\n- The supplied UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage sources list no inscriptions for the Democratic Republic of the Congo concerning the Mangbetu.\n",
